## Progress on `cache_coherency` (the last failing ship criterion)

**Reconciled state**: the ccloop session count and `sessNN_lessons` numbering were out of sync; sess96 (`FB9573F7`) was the real head, with the typeflip fix intact. Built on that.

### WIN 1 — Writer-side FIXED (verified, KEEP): publish-before-notify at commit
Root (sess96, proven): peers/the owner durably lost dir updates because a node modified its dir block from a **pinned-stale buffer** (DIR-STALE-SKIP). Two prior model consults converged on a write-side fix. I wired the long-dormant `mxfs_dlm_dir_durable_signal(dp)` (log_force + `xfs_bwrite`+wait_unpin each dir block + notify) into `xfs_remove`/`xfs_create`/`xfs_rename`, **post-commit with ILOCK+EX still held** — safe there (buffer holds only this node's own change) where sess96's release-time flush clobbered.
- **Result: `rename_visibility` 2/240 → 0 fails, PASS, 32s. DIR-STALE-SKIP eliminated.** The on-disk medium + owner are now always correct.

### WIN 2 (partial) — Reader-side: consumer eager dir-block refresh
Proved via remount that the remaining `unlink_visibility` failure is **pure peer in-core staleness** (medium clean). The lazy per-buffer hook restamps a block's gen *before* the reread, caching stale-as-current. Got a GPT design (durable per-dir seqlock epoch — saved to memory). Implemented the contained first step: `mxfs_dlm_dir_consumer_refresh()` at readdir/lookup entry eagerly evicts all clean dir blocks when a peer modified the dir (`i_dlm_dir_gen` advanced).
- **Result: unlink survivors 2 → 1.** Found the last-survivor cause: an undurable block was skipped by the evict but `evicted_gen` still advanced, so it was never retried.

### Built but NOT YET TESTED: `1B611843`
Fix for the last survivor: `mxfs_dir_evict_data_blocks` now returns success; `consumer_refresh` advances `evicted_gen` **only when every block was evicted**, so a skipped (transiently-pinned) block is retried on the next read.

